ET
BT
/F1 11 Tf
1 0 0 1 54 556 Tm
(1. the Linux kernel defines security hook points) Tj
ET
BT
/F1 11 Tf
1 0 0 1 54 535 Tm
(2. security modules attach logic to those hook points) Tj
ET
BT
/F1 11 Tf
1 0 0 1 54 514 Tm
(3. when a protected operation happens, the kernel calls that logic) Tj
ET
BT
/F1 11 Tf
1 0 0 1 54 493 Tm
(4. the security logic allows or denies the operation) Tj
ET
BT
/F1 11 Tf
1 0 0 1 54 472 Tm
(Examples of LSM-based systems include AppArmor, SELinux, Landlock, and BPF-LSM programs.) Tj
ET
BT
/F1 11 Tf
1 0 0 1 54 447 Tm
(Why it matters in Guardian:) Tj
ET
BT
/F1 11 Tf
1 0 0 1 54 422 Tm
(- Guardian uses the LSM framework as the kernel enforcement path) Tj
ET
BT
/F1 11 Tf
1 0 0 1 54 401 Tm
(- this is where file and exec blocking happen) Tj
ET
BT
/F1 11 Tf
1 0 0 1 54 380 Tm
(- moving more of Guardian's decision-making into LSM is a key part of the future) Tj
ET
BT
/F1 11 Tf
1 0 0 1 54 365 Tm
